# POST /imap/provision — step 0: who may call it.
#
# The route is open on loopback (the demo) and closed the moment a
# password is set: `txco auth tenant secrets set IMAP_PROVISION_PASSWORD
# --tenant <slug>` for the tenant that runs this stack, then callers send
# `Authorization: Basic base64("provision:<password>")` —
# `curl -u provision:<password>`. The secret never reaches the envelope:
# txco://basic-auth-verify consumes it inside the op and emits only a
# verdict (@computed.basic_auth_ok) plus whether a password was present
# (@computed.basic_auth_configured). The comparison is constant-time.
#
# `secrets.password.optional = true` lets the op run when the secret is
# unset (the processor otherwise skips an op whose secret it cannot
# materialize), and `allow_unconfigured = true` is the DECISION, written
# here where it can be seen: unset ⇒ open. Two consequences to know:
# a misspelled secret name reads as "unset", and the reject rule in 095
# fires only on an explicit false — on a chassis without this op the
# verdict is absent and 095 still fires, so the route fails CLOSED
# there rather than open. Verify after deploying with a password set:
# a POST without credentials must answer 401.
WHEN @src == "http" && @web.req.method == "POST" && @web.req.url.path == "/imap/provision"
WITH user = "provision",
     secrets.password.secret = "IMAP_PROVISION_PASSWORD",
     secrets.password.optional = true,
     allow_unconfigured = true
  EXEC "txco://basic-auth-verify"
